{"step_index":124,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T02:00:21Z","input_tokens":3053,"cache_read_tokens":102402,"output_tokens":1593,"content":"Incorporating the **UI** directly into the subsidiary boundary completes the full-stack autonomy for the instance. \n\nHere is how the UI is bounded to a subsidiary:\n\n---\n\n### The Subsidiary UI Architecture\n\n```\n                                  [ Door :8090 / op-web :8080 ]\n                                                │\n                 ┌──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┐\n                 ▼                              ▼                              ▼\n        [ Global / Root UI ]            [ Personal UI ]            [ Subsidiary UI (hack:3tched) ]\n        • Landing: `/`                  • Landing: `/personal`     • Landing: `/hack` (or custom port)\n        • Control-Plane Admin           • Personal Chats           • Custom SvelteKit / json-render Catalog\n        • System Health & Monitoring    • User Workspace           • Custom Tools, Branding & Prompt Panels\n```\n\n#### 1. Dedicated Landing Mount / Route (`LANDING=<subsidiary>`)\n`op-web` serves static UI bundles under `/usr/local/share/op-dbus/dashboard/` using named mounts:\n* **Host / Root Landing:** `URL_PREFIX=/` (default system dashboard).\n* **Subsidiary Landing:** `LANDING=hack` (served at `http://<host>:8080/hack/`).\n* The subsidiary can build and deploy its own customized Vite bundle from its repo (`npm run publish:op-web` with `LANDING=hack`) without touching the root dashboard.\n\n#### 2. Isolated `json-render` Catalog & Nav Manifest\nIn `@json-render/svelte`, the visible interface is driven by the active catalog and `NAV_MANIFEST`:\n* **Scoped Visibility:** Personal and system-admin sections (e.g., WireGuard keys, host kernel controls) are omitted.\n* **Domain-Specific Components:** The subsidiary registers only its own custom tool forms, hackathon prompt controls, and custom cards in its local registry.\n\n#### 3. OSCAL Taxonomy & Schema Representation\nIn the taxonomy, the UI definitions and events for the subsidiary carry explicit subids:\n* `sch.subsidiary.<name>.ui.manifest@v1` — The subsidiary's page specs and catalog definition.\n* `prj.subsidiary.<name>.ui.view@v1` — The projected dashboard view.\n* `evt.subsidiary.<name>.ui.interaction@v1` — User clicks and tool submissions.\n\n---\n\n### Complete Subsidiary Footprint\n\nWith UI included, every subsidiary instance (like `hack:3tched`) has 4 completely sovereign quadrants:\n\n| Layer | Dedicated Resource | Boundary Mechanism |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. UI / Frontend** | Custom Svelte bundle & Catalog | Dedicated landing mount (`/hack/`) or local dev port |\n| **2. Relational State** | Private Cozo Database | Dedicated ext4 image or user directory |\n| **3. Vector Memory** | Isolated Qdrant Collection | Namespaced collection (`subsidiary_hack_3tched`) |\n| **4. Event Bus** | Scoped EMQX MQTT Topics | `opdbus/subsidiary/hack_3tched/#` |\n\n---\n\n### Next Concrete Steps to Set This Up\n\n1. **Taxonomy Update:** Add `\"subsidiary\"` to [`INTERNAL_CATEGORIES`](file:///srv/git/odbus-verified/crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/common/oscal.rs#L45-L53) in `crates/op-plugins/src/state_plugins/common/oscal.rs`.\n2. **Instance Scaffold:** Set up the directory structure and config in `/home/jeremy/projects/hack-3tched` (or `/home/hack-3tched`) defining the Cozo path, Qdrant collection name, and UI landing prefix.\n\nReady to proceed with step 1?","thinking":"The user wants the user interface included within the specific instance's autonomy configuration, alongside the database and vector store settings. This implies a need to define the UI's role or characteristics within this instance's isolated environment. This suggests the UI is integral to the autonomy and should be configured alongside the other components.\n\n\nThe UI can be integrated through a dedicated mount point within the static web server, leveraging a named landing directory. Alternatively, a separate UI instance, potentially a fork, can be deployed. UI customization will be achieved through a dedicated navigation manifest within the rendering catalog, allowing subsidiary-specific page definitions and components tailored to its identity. Taxonomic mappings will categorize UI elements for the subsidiary.\n\n\n"}
